diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce the outage. From multiple office hosts (office_pc, enquiry, exam_cell, tpo) I successfully reached multiple machines in both labs: IT lab (192.168.1.3–.6) and CS lab (192.168.2.3–.6). All probes returned 0% loss with sub‑4 ms RTTs, confirming end‑to‑end L3 connectivity from the office to “the other lab.” Additionally, the campus routers currently advertise and select the correct routes: router1 (office/principal side) has RIP-learned routes to 192.168.1.0/24 and 192.168.2.0/24 via router0; router0 has routes back to 192.168.3.0/24 and 192.168.4.0/24 via router1; router2 propagates the rest. This indicates the routing subsystem (FRR/RIP on router0/1/2) and the inter‑router links are healthy now.

Mechanism-wise, the user’s symptoms would match a transient routing adjacency drop or a link flap on the office-to-core path (e.g., router1 eth0 or the WAN r0–r1), which would make “the other lab” momentarily unreachable from the office. However, no current evidence of such a fault is present.

Localization: Not applicable now; all tests pass across router1↔router0 and into both lab VLANs.

Root cause: Not currently determinable; the problem is not reproducible and paths/adjacencies are up.

Corrective fix: No change required. If this recurs, immediately capture “show ip route” and “show interface” on router1/router0, and enable short-term logging of interface link state and RIP adjacency events to pinpoint any transient flap. === END ===